Why GPLv3 (and not GPLv2, which HaulNX carried briefly):
Two features incorporate code under licenses that require it. The native RAR3
filter-decoder fallback (include/rar3.h, source/rar3/) ports the relevant slice
of the LGPLv3-licensed unarr project (github.com/zeniko/unarr); combining
LGPLv3 code into this project requires the combined work be conveyed under
GPLv3 terms. Separately, the embedded USB MTP responder's protocol/command
layer (include/mtp/, source/mtp/) follows the container-framing and dispatch
pattern used by cmtp-responder (github.com/cmtp-responder/cmtp-responder),
which is Apache-2.0 — a license that combines cleanly into a GPLv3 work but not
a GPLv2-only one. GPLv3 is the smallest license that legitimately
accommodates both, so the whole project moved there. HaulNX briefly carried
GPLv2 (relicensed from MIT) for an earlier version of the MTP responder that
adapted GPLv2-only code from Atmosphère's Haze; that code has since been
replaced with an independent implementation (see THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md), so
nothing in the current tree requires GPLv2 specifically.

Portions of source/rar3/ and include/rar3.h are:
  Copyright (c) 2015 the unarr project authors, used and adapted under the
  terms of the LGPLv3 — see licenses/LGPL-3.0.txt.

The container/dispatch pattern in include/mtp/ and source/mtp/ follows
cmtp-responder, Copyright (c) 2012, 2013 Samsung Electronics Co., Ltd. and
Copyright (c) 2019 Collabora Ltd, used under the terms of the Apache License,
version 2.0 — see licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.

Other bundled or linked components remain under their own, GPL-compatible
licenses (Plutonium and jsmn under MIT, the Noto Sans font subset under the SIL
Open Font License 1.1). Their notices are collected in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.
